\subsection{Phase D: source-global reset into the startup basin}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-d}

The almost-global qualification of the continuous gravity observer is a
property of smooth attitude flow on the sphere; it does not imply that the
implemented startup must inherit an arbitrary pre-existing attitude estimate.
The source explicitly seeds gravity direction from the first valid
accelerometer sample and initializes the private integral correction to zero.
Consequently the reset map can be analyzed before the continuous observer
flow.

Let $\widehat{\vct d}^{\,-}$ denote any stored gravity direction before startup,
including one arbitrarily close to the antipodal set.  At the first accepted
startup accelerometer sample write, as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-force-bound},
\begin{equation}
 -\vct f^B=g\vct d+\vct a_f,
 \qquad \|\vct a_f\|\le\bar f_0<g.
 \label{eq:widen-reset-force}
\end{equation}
Define the source gravity-reset map
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\vct d}^{\,+}
 :=\mathcal R_g(\vct f^B)
 :=\frac{-\vct f^B}{\|\vct f^B\|}.
 \label{eq:widen-gravity-reset}
\end{equation}
The reset depends on the current measurement and not on
$\widehat{\vct d}^{\,-}$.

\begin{lemma}[Prior-attitude-independent gravity reset]
\label{lem:widen-global-reset}
Under Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-force}, for every pre-reset attitude,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \angle(\widehat{\vct d}^{\,+},\vct d)
 \le\eta_{f,0}:=\sin^{-1}(\bar f_0/g)<\frac{\pi}{2}.}
 \label{eq:widen-global-reset-bound}
\end{equation}
Thus the reset image lies in the desired gravity hemisphere independently of
the pre-reset estimator attitude.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The vector $g\vct d+\vct a_f$ lies in the closed ball of radius $\bar f_0$
centered at $g\vct d$.  Because $\bar f_0<g$, the ball does not contain the
origin.  The largest angle subtended at the origin by that ball is the tangent
angle $\sin^{-1}(\bar f_0/g)$.  Normalization does not change the direction,
which proves Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-global-reset-bound}.  Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-gravity-reset}
contains no pre-reset attitude variable.
\end{proof}

The private startup observer also resets its integral correction, so if the
physical initial tangent gyro-bias error satisfies
$\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}(0)\|\le B_{\beta,0}$, the reset image obeys
\begin{equation}
 (\theta_P(0),\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}(0)\|)
 \in[0,\eta_{f,0}]\times[0,B_{\beta,0}].
 \label{eq:widen-reset-image}
\end{equation}
This is exactly the initial set entering the practical-ISS comparison of
Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit}.

\begin{theorem}[Source-global startup capture with respect to prior attitude]
\label{thm:widen-source-global-startup}
Assume Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-force}, the physical initial gyro-bias bound,
and the disturbance condition
\begin{equation}
 \max\{\bar V_{P,0}(\eta_{f,0},B_{\beta,0}),V_{P,\infty}\}
 <V_{P,\star}.
 \label{eq:widen-reset-chart-condition}
\end{equation}
Then, for every stored pre-startup attitude, the implemented gravity reset
places the private startup observer inside the quantitative chart of
Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit}.  The subsequent gravity-gate entry
time is bounded by Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time} with
$\theta_{P,0}^{\max}=\eta_{f,0}$.  If the remaining source hold, rate, tuner,
and heading-gauge gates complete, the handoff set is the same structured
$\mathcal H_{k_H}$ used by Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-live}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-global-reset} and the physical bias bound give
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-image} for every pre-reset attitude.  Condition
\eqref{eq:widen-reset-chart-condition} is the chart-invariance condition of the
existing startup theorem evaluated on that reset image.  The practical-ISS and
quality-gate comparisons therefore apply from the post-reset state, and all
subsequent source gates and Live handoff arguments are unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why this does not violate the attitude topological obstruction]
\label{rem:widen-reset-topology}
The theorem does not assert global asymptotic stability of a continuous
attitude observer.  It asserts globality only with respect to the discarded
\emph{pre-reset estimator attitude}: a discontinuous/source-triggered
measurement reset maps all such stored attitudes into one physical hemisphere.
The remaining quantitative result is still practical and conditional on the
specific-force, gyro-bias, and disturbance bounds.  Full-heading startup also
requires the later magnetic-gauge condition; accelerometer reset alone makes no
absolute-yaw claim.
\end{remark}
